IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `ucta-nouns`
IMPORT `ucta-logic`

-- Unfair Contract Terms Act 1977 (Singapore), 2020 Revised Edition, informal consolidation.
-- LAYER 1, MODULE 9: PART 1, OTHER PROVISIONS ABOUT CONTRACTS, sections 8 to 10
-- (Effect of breach; Evasion by means of secondary contract).
--
-- Section 8 reads "[Omitted in 1994 Revised Edition]": it is blank in this text and has no rule
-- (coverage row: repealed-or-blank).
--
-- READING A RESULT (see NOTES.md, "Reading a result: FALSE and NOTHING").
-- A caller must never turn NOTHING into FALSE.

§ `Section 9 - effect of breach`

-- src:292 | 9.—(1) Where for reliance upon it a contract term has to satisfy the
-- src:293 | requirement of reasonableness, it may be found to do so and be given
-- src:294 | effect accordingly notwithstanding that the contract has been
-- src:295 | terminated either by breach or by a party electing to treat it as
-- src:296 | repudiated.
@ref s 9(1) (src:292-296)
GIVEN `for reliance upon it the contract term has to satisfy the requirement of reasonableness` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the contract has been terminated, either by breach or by a party electing to treat it as repudiated` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 9(1) the contract term may be found to satisfy the requirement of reasonableness and be given effect` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT `for reliance upon it the contract term has to satisfy the requirement of reasonableness`
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words; marginal reading for a lawyer, a failed "where" on a positive grant is the text's no, ruling of 2026-11-05): "Where for reliance upon it a contract term has to satisfy the requirement of reasonableness, it may be found to do so and be given effect accordingly" -- the term need not satisfy it
        THEN JUST FALSE
        IF NOT `the contract has been terminated, either by breach or by a party electing to treat it as repudiated`
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): subsection (1) speaks of a contract that "has been terminated either by breach or by a party electing to treat it as repudiated"; for a contract still in force it says nothing
        THEN NOTHING
        -- TRUE-kind (the text says yes in its own words): "it may be found to do so and be given effect accordingly notwithstanding that the contract has been terminated either by breach or by a party electing to treat it as repudiated"
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

-- src:298 | (2) Where on a breach the contract is nevertheless affirmed by a
-- src:299 | party entitled to treat it as repudiated, this does not of itself exclude
-- src:300 | the requirement of reasonableness in relation to any contract term.
@ref s 9(2) (src:298-300)
GIVEN `there was a breach` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the contract is nevertheless affirmed by a party entitled to treat it as repudiated` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 9(2) affirming the contract of itself excludes the requirement of reasonableness in relation to the contract term` MEANS
    IF `there was a breach` AND `the contract is nevertheless affirmed by a party entitled to treat it as repudiated`
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Where on a breach the contract is nevertheless affirmed by a party entitled to treat it as repudiated, this does not of itself exclude the requirement of reasonableness in relation to any contract term"
    THEN JUST FALSE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): subsection (2) speaks of a breach and of affirmation by a party entitled to treat the contract as repudiated; outside that "Where" it does not say that affirming DOES exclude the requirement -- the question in its own words is `s 9(2) affirming the contract does not of itself exclude the requirement of reasonableness in relation to the contract term`, which answers JUST FALSE outside the "Where" (fork F6, site s 9(2))
    ELSE NOTHING

-- src:298 | (2) Where on a breach the contract is nevertheless affirmed by a
-- src:299 | party entitled to treat it as repudiated, this does not of itself exclude
-- src:300 | the requirement of reasonableness in relation to any contract term.
@ref s 9(2), the provision applies (src:298-300)
GIVEN `there was a breach` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the contract is nevertheless affirmed by a party entitled to treat it as repudiated` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 9(2) affirming the contract does not of itself exclude the requirement of reasonableness in relation to the contract term` MEANS
    IF `there was a breach` AND `the contract is nevertheless affirmed by a party entitled to treat it as repudiated`
    -- TRUE-kind (the text says yes in its own words): "Where on a breach the contract is nevertheless affirmed by a party entitled to treat it as repudiated, this does not of itself exclude the requirement of reasonableness in relation to any contract term"
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words; marginal reading for a lawyer, a failed "Where" on a negative statement is the text's no, ruling of 2026-11-05 (b)): there was no breach, or the contract was not affirmed by a party entitled to treat it as repudiated, so the subsection does not apply
    ELSE JUST FALSE

§ `Section 10 - evasion by means of secondary contract`

-- src:304 | 10. A person is not bound by any contract term prejudicing or
-- src:305 | taking away rights of his which arise under, or in connection with the
-- src:306 | performance of, another contract, so far as those rights extend to the
-- src:307 | enforcement of another’s liability which this Part prevents that other
-- src:308 | from excluding or restricting.
@ref s 10 (src:304-308)
GIVEN `the term prejudices or takes away rights of the person which arise under, or in connection with the performance of, another contract` IS A BOOLEAN
      `those rights extend to the enforcement of another's liability` IS A BOOLEAN
      `this Part prevents that other from excluding or restricting that liability` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 10 the person is bound by the contract term` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT `the term prejudices or takes away rights of the person which arise under, or in connection with the performance of, another contract`
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "any contract term prejudicing or taking away rights of his which arise under, or in connection with the performance of, another contract"
        THEN NOTHING
        IF NOT `those rights extend to the enforcement of another's liability`
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "so far as those rights extend to the enforcement of another's liability"
        THEN NOTHING
        IF `this Part prevents that other from excluding or restricting that liability` EQUALS JUST TRUE
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "A person is not bound by any contract term prejudicing or taking away rights of his ... so far as those rights extend to the enforcement of another's liability which this Part prevents that other from excluding or restricting"
        THEN JUST FALSE
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): this Part does not prevent the other from excluding or restricting the liability, or that is not known (the "so far as" limit); the section says nothing
        OTHERWISE NOTHING
